<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Men of Harlech</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">tune first published <a href="/years/1794">1794</a>; first printed with words c. 1830</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr Harlech</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>&quot;, or &quot;The March of the Men of Harlech&quot; (in Welsh &quot;Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr Harlech&quot;), is a song and military march traditionally said to tell of the siege of Harlech Castle from 1461 to 1468, when the castle was held for the Lancastrians against the Yorkists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>.</p></div>
